section.section_3_4
    h2 The Perils of Psychohistory
    p
        |Reducing the complexity of the biographical subject to a single explanatory key — in
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |&#32;
        |case, the psychohistorians have proposed parental abuse — impoverishes explanation. Most historians have been suspicious of a method that leaves so much out — culture and context, politics and ideas — and renders stated motivations suspect, reducing them to psychological functions (rationalization, compensation, sublimation).
    p
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |&#32;
        |boyhood friend,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="IoIr") Ioseb Iremashvili
        |, later a political opponent, composed the first memoir of the Soviet ruler’s childhood, where he made the primary psychological deduction later followed by other biographers:
    p Undeserved, terrible beatings made the boy as hard and heartless as his father himself. Since all men who had authority over others either through power or age reminded him of his father, there soon arose a feeling of revenge against all men who stood above him. From his youth the realization of his thoughts of revenge became the goal toward which everything was aimed.
    p
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |evidently had a different evaluation of the disciplining practiced by his parents. When the popular German biographer
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="EmLu") Emil Ludwig
        |, known for his interviews with the political celebrities of the interwar years, met with
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |in the
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plKr") Kremlin
        |, he indelicately broached the issue of parental abuse: “What pushed you into opposition? Perhaps the bad treatment by your parents?”
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |did not take the bait, however. “No,” he answered. “My parents were uneducated people, but they treated me not badly at all.”